    "summary": "Discover what Python is, why it is so popular, how to run code in the Bargisy eSchool Python sandbox, and the design philosophy behind clean Python code.",
    "content": "# Lesson 01: Introduction to Python, the Browser Sandbox & The Zen of Python\n\nPython is one of the most popular programming languages in the world. It is used for automation, data analysis, websites, artificial intelligence and much more - and it is famous for being **easy to read**. In this course you will write real Python 3 code directly in your browser.\n\n## 1. Why Python?\n- **Readable**: Python code looks almost like English.\n- **Versatile**: automate Excel reports, analyse sales data, build web apps, train AI models.\n- **Huge community**: thousands of free libraries (pandas, Django, NumPy...).\n- **Beginner-friendly**: you can write useful programs on day one.\n\n## 2. Running Python in this course\nEvery code example has a **\"Try this Python in the Sandbox\"** button. It opens the **Interactive Sandbox** with the code loaded and runs it with a real Python 3 engine (Pyodide). You can also open *Interactive Sandbox > Python* from the menu and type your own code. Press **Run** or **Ctrl + Enter**.\n\nThe first run downloads the Python engine (a few seconds); after that it is instant.\n\n## 3. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Your first program\n```python\nprint(\"Hello, Bargisy eSchool!\")\nprint(\"Python is running in my browser.\")\n```\nOutput:\n```text\nHello, Bargisy eSchool!\nPython is running in my browser.\n```\n\n### Example 2: Python as a calculator\n```python\nprint(250 * 12)        # monthly to yearly\nprint(1250 * 0.15)     # 15% VAT\nprint((80 + 95 + 70) / 3)  # average\n```\nOutput: `3000`, `187.5`, `81.66666666666667`.\n\n### Example 3: The Zen of Python\nPython has a built-in poem describing its philosophy:\n```python\nimport this\n```\nSome key lines: *\"Beautiful is better than ugly.\" \"Simple is better than complex.\" \"Readability counts.\"* These ideas guide how good Python code is written.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Make mistakes on purpose! Remove a bracket and run the code - reading error messages is one of the most important programming skills.\n\n## 4. Common mistakes\n- **Capital letters**: `Print(\"hi\")` fails - Python is case-sensitive; it must be `print`.\n- **Missing quotes** around text: `print(Hello)` -> `NameError`.\n- **Curly \"smart\" quotes** copied from Word (`“ ”`) - use straight quotes `\" \"`.\n\n## 5. Hands-on Student Challenge\nWrite a program that prints:\n1. Your name.\n2. The city you live in.\n3. The result of 365 x 24 (hours in a year).\n4. The first two lines of the Zen of Python (use `import this` and copy them into print statements).\n\n:::solution\n```python\nprint(\"Mohammed Bargisy\")\nprint(\"Jeddah\")\nprint(365 * 24)\nprint(\"Beautiful is better than ugly.\")\nprint(\"Explicit is better than implicit.\")\n```\nOutput:\n```text\nMohammed Bargisy\nJeddah\n8760\nBeautiful is better than ugly.\nExplicit is better than implicit.\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- Python is readable, versatile and beginner-friendly.\n- `print()` shows output; Python can calculate directly.\n- Use the sandbox button on every example to run and experiment.\n",
