    "title": "Lesson 02: Your First Script: print(), Comments & Clean Formatting",
    "slug": "python-lesson-02-your-first-script-print-comments-clean-formatting",
    "category": "programming",
    "track": "python",
    "track_name": "Python Path",
    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Master the print() function, single-line and multi-line comments, and PEP 8 formatting rules.",
    "content": "# Lesson 02: Your First Script: print(), Comments & Clean Formatting\n\nEvery program needs to show results and every good program needs explanations for humans. In this lesson you master `print()` and **comments**, and learn the formatting habits that make code easy to read.\n\n## 1. The print() function\n`print()` displays values. You can print text, numbers and several values at once:\n```python\nprint(\"Sales report\")\nprint(1500)\nprint(\"Total:\", 1500, \"SAR\")     # separated by spaces\nprint()                           # empty line\n```\n\n### Useful options\n- `sep` - what goes between values (default: a space).\n- `end` - what goes at the end (default: a new line `\\n`).\n```python\nprint(\"2026\", \"11\", \"17\", sep=\"-\")   # 2026-11-17\nprint(\"Loading\", end=\"...\")\nprint(\"done\")                         # Loading...done\n```\n\n## 2. Comments\nA comment starts with `#`. Python ignores it - it is for people.\n```python\n# Calculate VAT for one invoice\nprice = 1000\nvat = price * 0.15   # Saudi VAT is 15%\nprint(vat)\n```\nGood comments explain **why**, not what is obvious.\n\nMulti-line notes are often written as a **docstring** (triple quotes) at the top of a file:\n```python\n\"\"\"\nMonthly sales script\nAuthor: Mohammed\n\"\"\"\nprint(\"Running...\")\n```\n\n## 3. Worked examples\n### Example 1: A receipt\n```python\nprint(\"=\" * 30)\nprint(\"BARGISY PHARMACY RECEIPT\")\nprint(\"=\" * 30)\nprint(\"Eye drops\", \"x2\", \"50 SAR\", sep=\"   \")\nprint(\"Gel\", \"x1\", \"40 SAR\", sep=\"   \")\nprint(\"-\" * 30)\nprint(\"TOTAL:\", 90, \"SAR\")\n```\n`\"=\" * 30` repeats the character 30 times - a neat trick for lines.\n\n### Example 2: Escape characters\n```python\nprint(\"Line 1\\nLine 2\")       # \\n = new line\nprint(\"Name:\\tAhmed\")         # \\t = tab\nprint(\"He said \\\"hello\\\"\")    # \\\" = quote inside text\n```\n\n## 4. Clean formatting (PEP 8)\n- One statement per line.\n- Spaces around operators: `total = price * qty`, not `total=price*qty`.\n- Lines under ~79-100 characters.\n- 4 spaces for indentation (Lesson 17).\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Comment out a line (put `#` in front) to temporarily disable it while testing.\n\n## 5. Common mistakes\n- **Comments with `//`** (that's JavaScript) - Python uses `#`.\n- **Forgetting the brackets**: `print \"hi\"` is Python 2 - Python 3 needs `print(\"hi\")`.\n- **Mixing text and numbers with `+`**: `print(\"Total: \" + 90)` -> TypeError. Use a comma or f-strings (Lesson 8).\n\n## 6. Hands-on Student Challenge\nWrite a program that prints this business card exactly, using `\"*\" * 32` for the borders and `sep`/`end` at least once:\n```text\n********************************\nMohammed Bargisy\nArea Sales Manager\nJeddah | 2026\n********************************\n```\n\n:::solution\n```python\n# Business card printer\nborder = \"*\" * 32\nprint(border)\nprint(\"Mohammed Bargisy\")\nprint(\"Area Sales Manager\")\nprint(\"Jeddah\", \"2026\", sep=\" | \")   # uses sep\nprint(border, end=\"\\n\")              # end (default value shown)\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- `print()` shows values; `sep` and `end` control spacing.\n- `#` starts a comment; explain *why* in comments.\n- Follow simple formatting rules for readable code.\n",
