    "id": "py-4",
    "lesson_number": 4,
    "title": "Lesson 04: Numbers: Integers, Floating-Point Precision & Complex Numbers",
    "slug": "python-lesson-04-numbers-integers-floating-point-precision-complex-numbers",
    "category": "programming",
    "track": "python",
    "track_name": "Python Path",
    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Understand integer representation, floating-point arithmetic precision, and scientific notation in Python.",
    "content": "# Lesson 04: Numbers: Integers, Floating-Point Precision & Complex Numbers\n\nPython has three numeric types: **int** (whole numbers), **float** (decimals) and **complex** (for advanced maths). Understanding floats is important because computers store decimals in a way that sometimes surprises people.\n\n## 1. Integers and floats\n```python\nunits = 120          # int\nprice = 39.99        # float\nprint(type(units), type(price))\nprint(10 / 2)        # 5.0  -> division always gives a float\nprint(10 // 2)       # 5    -> floor division gives an int\n```\nLarge numbers can use underscores for readability: `budget = 1_500_000`.\n\n## 2. Floating-point precision\n```python\nprint(0.1 + 0.2)          # 0.30000000000000004\nprint(0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3)   # False!\n```\nComputers store decimals in binary, so some values are tiny bit off. Solutions:\n```python\nprint(round(0.1 + 0.2, 2))          # 0.3\nimport math\nprint(math.isclose(0.1 + 0.2, 0.3)) # True\n```\nFor money, the `decimal` module is exact:\n```python\nfrom decimal import Decimal\nprint(Decimal(\"0.1\") + Decimal(\"0.2\"))   # 0.3\n```\n\n## 3. Complex numbers\n```python\nz = 3 + 4j\nprint(z.real, z.imag, abs(z))   # 3.0 4.0 5.0\n```\nUsed in engineering and science - rarely in business code.\n\n## 4. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Useful number functions\n```python\nprint(abs(-250))        # 250\nprint(round(3.14159, 2))# 3.14\nprint(max(4, 9, 2))     # 9\nprint(min(4, 9, 2))     # 2\nprint(pow(2, 10))       # 1024\n```\n\n### Example 2: Money with Decimal\n```python\nfrom decimal import Decimal, ROUND_HALF_UP\nprice = Decimal(\"19.995\")\nprint(price.quantize(Decimal(\"0.01\"), rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP))   # 20.00\n```\n\n### Example 3: Converting\n```python\nprint(int(7.9))      # 7   (cuts decimals)\nprint(float(7))      # 7.0\nprint(int(\"42\") + 1) # 43\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Python's `round()` uses \"banker's rounding\": `round(2.5)` is `2`, `round(3.5)` is `4`. Use `Decimal` with `ROUND_HALF_UP` when you need classic rounding for invoices.\n\n## 5. Common mistakes\n- **Comparing floats with `==`.**\n- **Expecting `/` to return an int.**\n- **Using floats for exact money totals** across many transactions.\n\n## 6. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Store 3 prices: 19.99, 5.01 and 0.1 + 0.2. Print their sum and the sum rounded to 2 decimals.\n2. Check whether `0.1 * 3` equals `0.3` using `==` and `math.isclose`.\n3. Calculate 15% VAT on 99.95 using `Decimal` and round half up to 2 decimals.\n\n:::solution\n```python\nimport math\nfrom decimal import Decimal, ROUND_HALF_UP\n\ntotal = 19.99 + 5.01 + (0.1 + 0.2)\nprint(total)               # 25.300000000000004 (approximately)\nprint(round(total, 2))     # 25.3\n\nprint(0.1 * 3 == 0.3)               # False\nprint(math.isclose(0.1 * 3, 0.3))   # True\n\nvat = Decimal(\"99.95\") * Decimal(\"0.15\")\nprint(vat)                                                   # 14.9925\nprint(vat.quantize(Decimal(\"0.01\"), rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP)) # 14.99\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- int = whole numbers, float = decimals; `/` always returns a float.\n- Floats can be slightly imprecise - use round, math.isclose or Decimal.\n- Decimal is the safe choice for money.\n",
