    "title": "Lesson 05: Arithmetic Operators: +, -, *, /, Floor Division, Modulo, and Exponents",
    "slug": "python-lesson-05-arithmetic-operators-floor-division-modulo-and-exponents",
    "category": "programming",
    "track": "python",
    "track_name": "Python Path",
    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Master Python's arithmetic operators including true division (/), floor division (//), modulo (%), and power (**).",
    "content": "# Lesson 05: Arithmetic Operators: +, -, *, /, Floor Division, Modulo, and Exponents\n\nArithmetic operators are the tools for calculations - totals, averages, remainders and growth.\n\n## 1. The operators\n\n| Operator | Meaning | Example | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `+` | Add | `7 + 2` | 9 |\n| `-` | Subtract | `7 - 2` | 5 |\n| `*` | Multiply | `7 * 2` | 14 |\n| `/` | Divide | `7 / 2` | 3.5 |\n| `//` | Floor division | `7 // 2` | 3 |\n| `%` | Modulo (remainder) | `7 % 2` | 1 |\n| `**` | Power | `7 ** 2` | 49 |\n\nOrder of operations: `**` first, then `* / // %`, then `+ -`. Use brackets to be clear.\n\n## 2. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Boxes and leftovers\n```python\ntablets = 1045\nper_box = 100\nfull_boxes = tablets // per_box   # 10\nleftover = tablets % per_box      # 45\nprint(full_boxes, \"boxes and\", leftover, \"tablets left\")\n```\n\n### Example 2: Even or odd\n```python\nn = 17\nprint(n % 2 == 0)   # False -> odd\n```\n\n### Example 3: Compound growth\nSales of 10,000 growing 8% per year for 3 years:\n```python\nsales = 10000 * (1 + 0.08) ** 3\nprint(round(sales, 2))   # 12597.12\n```\n\n### Example 4: Minutes to hours and minutes\n```python\nminutes = 2750\nhours, mins = divmod(minutes, 60)   # divmod returns both at once\nprint(hours, \"h\", mins, \"min\")      # 45 h 50 min\n```\n\n### Example 5: Augmented assignment\n```python\ntotal = 0\ntotal += 250   # total = total + 250\ntotal *= 2     # 500\ntotal -= 100   # 400\nprint(total)\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> `%` is perfect for \"every n-th\" logic: `if i % 5 == 0:` runs every 5th item.\n\n## 3. Common mistakes\n- **Using `^` for power** - in Python `^` is XOR. Use `**`.\n- **Forgetting brackets**: `(a + b) / 2` vs `a + b / 2`.\n- **Division by zero** -> `ZeroDivisionError`.\n\n## 4. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. A trip is 1,000 km; a car uses 7.5 L per 100 km; fuel costs 2.33 SAR/L. Calculate litres needed and cost.\n2. 500 students need buses of 45 seats. How many full buses, how many students in the last bus, and how many buses in total?\n3. Calculate 2 to the power of 16.\n\n:::solution\n```python\n# 1. Fuel\ndistance = 1000\nlitres = distance / 100 * 7.5\ncost = litres * 2.33\nprint(\"Litres:\", litres)            # 75.0\nprint(\"Cost:\", round(cost, 2))      # 174.75\n\n# 2. Buses\nstudents = 500\nseats = 45\nfull_buses = students // seats      # 11\nlast_bus = students % seats         # 5\ntotal_buses = full_buses + (1 if last_bus > 0 else 0)   # 12\nprint(full_buses, last_bus, total_buses)\n\n# 3. Power\nprint(2 ** 16)                      # 65536\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- `//` floor division and `%` remainder solve \"how many and how many left\".\n- `**` is power; `divmod` gives quotient and remainder together.\n- Use brackets to control the order of operations.\n",
