    "summary": "Learn how to create strings using single, double, and triple quotes, and handle escape characters like \\n and \\t.",
    "content": "# Lesson 06: Strings Basics: Quotes, Escape Sequences & Multiline Blocks\n\nText in Python is called a **string** (`str`). Names, messages, product codes, emails - almost every program works with strings.\n\n## 1. Creating strings\n```python\na = 'single quotes'\nb = \"double quotes\"\nc = \"It's easy\"               # use double quotes when text contains '\nd = 'He said \"hello\"'         # and the opposite\n```\n\n## 2. Escape sequences\n\n| Sequence | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `\\n` | New line |\n| `\\t` | Tab |\n| `\\\\` | Backslash |\n| `\\'` / `\\\"` | Quote inside quotes |\n\n```python\nprint(\"Name:\\tAhmed\\nCity:\\tJeddah\")\n```\n\n**Raw strings** ignore escapes - useful for Windows paths:\n```python\npath = r\"C:\\Users\\new_folder\\test\"\nprint(path)\n```\n\n## 3. Multiline strings\nTriple quotes keep line breaks:\n```python\nmessage = \"\"\"Dear customer,\nYour order has been shipped.\nThank you!\"\"\"\nprint(message)\n```\n\n## 4. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Joining and repeating\n```python\nfirst = \"Mohammed\"\nlast = \"Bargisy\"\nfull = first + \" \" + last\nprint(full)              # Mohammed Bargisy\nprint(\"-\" * 20)          # --------------------\n```\n\n### Example 2: Length\n```python\ncode = \"JED-2026-0451\"\nprint(len(code))         # 13\n```\n\n### Example 3: Strings are immutable\n```python\ns = \"hello\"\n# s[0] = \"H\"   -> TypeError\ns = \"H\" + s[1:]          # create a new string instead\nprint(s)                 # Hello\n```\n\n### Example 4: Arabic text works too\n```python\ngreeting = \"مرحبا بكم\"\nprint(greeting, len(greeting))\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Python 3 strings are Unicode - Arabic, emoji and accented letters all work out of the box.\n\n## 5. Common mistakes\n- **Mismatched quotes**: `\"hello'` -> SyntaxError.\n- **Adding numbers to strings**: `\"Total: \" + 5` -> TypeError. Use `str(5)` or f-strings.\n- **Windows paths with single backslashes** like `\"C:\\new\"` (`\\n` becomes a new line).\n\n## 6. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Print a 3-line address using one string with `\\n`.\n2. Print the same address using a triple-quoted string.\n3. Print the Windows path `C:\\Reports\\november\\total.xlsx` correctly.\n4. Print the length of your full name.\n\n:::solution\n```python\nprint(\"Bargisy eSchool\\nKing Road, Al Rawdah\\nJeddah 23435\")\n\naddress = \"\"\"Bargisy eSchool\nKing Road, Al Rawdah\nJeddah 23435\"\"\"\nprint(address)\n\nprint(r\"C:\\Reports\\november\\total.xlsx\")   # raw string keeps backslashes\n\nfull_name = \"Mohammed Bargisy\"\nprint(len(full_name))   # 16\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- Strings use single, double or triple quotes.\n- `\\n`, `\\t` and raw strings `r\"...\"` control special characters.\n- Strings are immutable; build new strings instead of changing them.\n",
