    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Check item existence, locate first matching indices, and tally frequencies in lists.",
    "content": "# Lesson 24: Searching & Counting in Lists: in, .index(), and .count()\n\nBefore working with an item, you often need to know: *Is it in the list? Where is it? How many times does it appear?*\n\n## 1. The tools\n\n| Tool | Answers | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `x in lst` | Is x present? | `\"Gel\" in products` |\n| `x not in lst` | Is x missing? | `\"Spray\" not in products` |\n| `lst.index(x)` | Position of the first x (ValueError if missing) | `products.index(\"Gel\")` |\n| `lst.count(x)` | How many times x appears | `regions.count(\"Jeddah\")` |\n\n## 2. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Membership\n```python\napproved = [\"Al Nahdi\", \"Al Dawaa\", \"United\"]\ncustomer = \"Whites\"\nif customer in approved:\n    print(\"Approved customer\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Requires approval\")\n```\n\n### Example 2: Position\n```python\nmonths = [\"Jan\", \"Feb\", \"Mar\", \"Apr\"]\nprint(months.index(\"Mar\"))       # 2\nprint(months.index(\"Mar\") + 1)   # 3 -> month number\n```\n\n### Example 3: Counting\n```python\nregions = [\"Jeddah\", \"Makkah\", \"Jeddah\", \"Taif\", \"Jeddah\"]\nprint(regions.count(\"Jeddah\"))   # 3\n```\n\n### Example 4: Count every value\n```python\nregions = [\"Jeddah\", \"Makkah\", \"Jeddah\", \"Taif\", \"Jeddah\"]\nfor r in sorted(set(regions)):\n    print(r, regions.count(r))\n```\nOutput:\n```text\nJeddah 3\nMakkah 1\nTaif 1\n```\n(`collections.Counter` does this even better - Lesson 95.)\n\n### Example 5: Safe index\n```python\nproducts = [\"Gel\", \"Drops\"]\nname = \"Spray\"\nposition = products.index(name) if name in products else -1\nprint(position)   # -1\n```\n\n### Example 6: Finding the best rep\n```python\nreps = [\"Ahmed\", \"Sara\", \"Omar\"]\nsales = [14000, 9000, 16000]\nbest = reps[sales.index(max(sales))]\nprint(best)       # Omar\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Searching with `in` on a long **list** checks items one by one. For thousands of lookups, use a **set** or **dictionary** (Lessons 35, 39) - they are much faster.\n\n## 3. Common mistakes\n- **`index` on a missing value** -> ValueError.\n- **Case differences**: `\"gel\" in [\"Gel\"]` is False.\n- **Calling `count` inside a big loop** - slow on large lists.\n\n## 4. Hands-on Student Challenge\n```python\nvisits = [\"Al Nahdi\", \"United\", \"Al Nahdi\", \"Whites\", \"Al Dawaa\", \"Al Nahdi\", \"United\"]\n```\n1. Was \"Whites\" visited? Was \"Lemon\" visited?\n2. On which visit number (1-based) was \"Al Dawaa\" first visited?\n3. How many times was each customer visited? (sorted by name)\n4. Which customer was visited most?\n\n:::solution\n```python\nvisits = [\"Al Nahdi\", \"United\", \"Al Nahdi\", \"Whites\", \"Al Dawaa\", \"Al Nahdi\", \"United\"]\n\nprint(\"Whites\" in visits, \"Lemon\" in visits)     # True False\nprint(visits.index(\"Al Dawaa\") + 1)              # 5\n\ncustomers = sorted(set(visits))\nfor c in customers:\n    print(c, visits.count(c))\n# Al Dawaa 1 / Al Nahdi 3 / United 2 / Whites 1\n\nmost = max(customers, key=visits.count)\nprint(\"Most visited:\", most)                     # Al Nahdi\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- `in` / `not in` test membership.\n- `index` finds the first position; `count` counts occurrences.\n- Combine with `max(..., key=...)` to find the most frequent item.\n",
    "tags": "Python, Lesson 24, Programming, Beginner",
