    "id": "py-38",
    "lesson_number": 38,
    "title": "Lesson 38: Nested Dictionaries & Complex Data Modeling",
    "slug": "python-lesson-38-nested-dictionaries-complex-data-modeling",
    "category": "programming",
    "track": "python",
    "track_name": "Python Path",
    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Model real-world entities with hierarchies of nested dictionaries and lists.",
    "content": "# Lesson 38: Nested Dictionaries & Complex Data Modeling\n\nReal data is structured: customers have branches, branches have monthly sales, products have details. **Nested dictionaries** (and lists inside dicts) model this naturally - it's also exactly how JSON data looks.\n\n## 1. Nested structures\n```python\ncustomer = {\n    \"name\": \"Al Nahdi\",\n    \"city\": \"Jeddah\",\n    \"contacts\": {\"phone\": \"0501234567\", \"email\": \"info@nahdi.sa\"},\n    \"branches\": [\"Rawdah\", \"Tahlia\", \"Corniche\"],\n}\nprint(customer[\"contacts\"][\"email\"])   # info@nahdi.sa\nprint(customer[\"branches\"][0])         # Rawdah\nprint(len(customer[\"branches\"]))       # 3\n```\n\n## 2. A dict of dicts\n```python\nreps = {\n    \"Ahmed\": {\"region\": \"Jeddah\", \"target\": 12000, \"sales\": 14000},\n    \"Sara\": {\"region\": \"Makkah\", \"target\": 10000, \"sales\": 9000},\n}\nfor name, info in reps.items():\n    pct = info[\"sales\"] / info[\"target\"]\n    print(f\"{name} ({info['region']}): {pct:.0%}\")\n```\n\n## 3. A list of dicts (records)\n```python\norders = [\n    {\"id\": 101, \"customer\": \"Al Nahdi\", \"amount\": 4500, \"paid\": True},\n    {\"id\": 102, \"customer\": \"Al Dawaa\", \"amount\": 1200, \"paid\": False},\n    {\"id\": 103, \"customer\": \"Al Nahdi\", \"amount\": 2300, \"paid\": False},\n]\nunpaid = [o for o in orders if not o[\"paid\"]]\nprint(sum(o[\"amount\"] for o in unpaid))   # 3500\n```\n\n## 4. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Safe deep access\n```python\ncustomer = {\"name\": \"United\", \"contacts\": {}}\nemail = customer.get(\"contacts\", {}).get(\"email\", \"no email\")\nprint(email)    # no email\n```\n\n### Example 2: Grouping into a nested dict\n```python\norders = [(\"Jeddah\", \"Gel\", 3), (\"Jeddah\", \"Drops\", 5), (\"Makkah\", \"Gel\", 2)]\nsummary = {}\nfor region, product, qty in orders:\n    summary.setdefault(region, {})\n    summary[region][product] = summary[region].get(product, 0) + qty\nprint(summary)  # {'Jeddah': {'Gel': 3, 'Drops': 5}, 'Makkah': {'Gel': 2}}\n```\n\n### Example 3: Pretty printing\n```python\nimport pprint\npprint.pprint({\"Jeddah\": {\"Gel\": 3, \"Drops\": 5}, \"Makkah\": {\"Gel\": 2}}, width=40)\n```\n\n### Example 4: Updating nested values\n```python\nreps = {\"Ahmed\": {\"sales\": 14000}}\nreps[\"Ahmed\"][\"sales\"] += 500\nprint(reps)\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Design data as a **list of dicts** when you have many similar records (like rows in Excel), and as a **dict of dicts** when you look things up by a key (like a customer ID).\n\n## 5. Common mistakes\n- **KeyError deep inside** - use `.get()` with `{}` defaults.\n- **Shared inner objects** - copying the outer dict doesn't copy inner dicts (Lesson 97).\n- **Overly deep nesting** - more than 3 levels is hard to work with.\n\n## 6. Hands-on Student Challenge\nModel 3 pharmacies, each with a city, a list of products stocked, and a dict of monthly sales (`{\"Oct\": ..., \"Nov\": ...}`). Then:\n1. Print each pharmacy's total sales.\n2. Print pharmacies that stock \"Gel\".\n3. Find the pharmacy with the highest November sales.\n4. Add December sales of 5,000 to one pharmacy.\n\n:::solution\n```python\npharmacies = {\n    \"Al Nahdi Rawdah\": {\"city\": \"Jeddah\", \"products\": [\"Gel\", \"Drops\"], \"sales\": {\"Oct\": 4200, \"Nov\": 5100}},\n    \"Al Dawaa Aziziyah\": {\"city\": \"Makkah\", \"products\": [\"Drops\", \"Wipes\"], \"sales\": {\"Oct\": 3100, \"Nov\": 2900}},\n    \"United Tahlia\": {\"city\": \"Jeddah\", \"products\": [\"Gel\", \"Wipes\", \"Spray\"], \"sales\": {\"Oct\": 3800, \"Nov\": 6200}},\n}\n\nfor name, info in pharmacies.items():\n    print(name, sum(info[\"sales\"].values()))\n# Al Nahdi Rawdah 9300 / Al Dawaa Aziziyah 6000 / United Tahlia 10000\n\nprint([n for n, info in pharmacies.items() if \"Gel\" in info[\"products\"]])\n# ['Al Nahdi Rawdah', 'United Tahlia']\n\nbest = max(pharmacies, key=lambda n: pharmacies[n][\"sales\"].get(\"Nov\", 0))\nprint(\"Best November:\", best)        # United Tahlia\n\npharmacies[\"Al Nahdi Rawdah\"][\"sales\"][\"Dec\"] = 5000\nprint(pharmacies[\"Al Nahdi Rawdah\"][\"sales\"])\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- Dicts and lists can be nested to model real structures.\n- Access with chained keys/indexes; use `.get()` for safety.\n- List of dicts = table rows; dict of dicts = lookup by key.\n",
    "tags": "Python, Lesson 38, Programming, Intermediate",
