    "id": "py-39",
    "lesson_number": 39,
    "title": "Lesson 39: Introduction to Sets: Uniqueness & Hashable Elements",
    "slug": "python-lesson-39-introduction-to-sets-uniqueness-hashable-elements",
    "category": "programming",
    "track": "python",
    "track_name": "Python Path",
    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Deduplicate data instantly and leverage set mathematics for lightning-fast membership lookups.",
    "content": "# Lesson 39: Introduction to Sets: Uniqueness & Hashable Elements\n\nA **set** is an unordered collection of **unique** values. Sets remove duplicates automatically and check membership extremely fast.\n\n## 1. Creating sets\n```python\nregions = {\"Jeddah\", \"Makkah\", \"Jeddah\", \"Taif\"}\nprint(regions)            # {'Jeddah', 'Makkah', 'Taif'} - duplicates gone (order may vary)\nempty = set()             # NOT {} - that's an empty dict!\nfrom_list = set([\"a\", \"b\", \"a\"])\nprint(len(from_list))     # 2\n```\n\n## 2. Hashable elements\nSet items must be **immutable** (hashable): numbers, strings, tuples. Lists and dicts can't go inside a set.\n\n## 3. Adding and removing\n```python\ns = {\"Gel\"}\ns.add(\"Drops\")\ns.update([\"Wipes\", \"Spray\"])\ns.discard(\"Spray\")        # no error if missing\ns.remove(\"Wipes\")         # KeyError if missing\nprint(s)\n```\n\n## 4. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Unique customers\n```python\nvisits = [\"Al Nahdi\", \"United\", \"Al Nahdi\", \"Whites\", \"United\"]\nunique = set(visits)\nprint(len(unique), sorted(unique))   # 3 ['Al Nahdi', 'United', 'Whites']\n```\n\n### Example 2: Fast membership\n```python\nblocked = {\"0500000000\", \"0555555555\"}\nphone = \"0555555555\"\nprint(phone in blocked)   # True - instant even for millions of items\n```\n\n### Example 3: Remove duplicates but keep order\n```python\nitems = [\"Gel\", \"Drops\", \"Gel\", \"Wipes\", \"Drops\"]\nprint(list(dict.fromkeys(items)))   # ['Gel', 'Drops', 'Wipes']\n```\n(A plain `set()` loses the original order.)\n\n### Example 4: Check for duplicates\n```python\nids = [101, 102, 103, 102]\nprint(len(ids) != len(set(ids)))    # True - there are duplicates\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Converting a list to a set and back is the quickest way to deduplicate - just remember sets don't keep order.\n\n## 5. Common mistakes\n- **`{}` for an empty set** - it creates a dict.\n- **Expecting order or indexing** - `s[0]` fails.\n- **Adding lists** to a set -> TypeError (use tuples).\n\n## 6. Hands-on Student Challenge\n```python\norders_jan = [\"Al Nahdi\", \"United\", \"Whites\", \"Al Nahdi\", \"Lemon\"]\n```\n1. How many distinct customers ordered in January?\n2. Print them alphabetically.\n3. Check whether \"Al Dawaa\" ordered.\n4. Remove duplicates from the list while keeping the original order.\n5. Add \"Al Dawaa\" to the set and remove \"Lemon\" safely.\n\n:::solution\n```python\norders_jan = [\"Al Nahdi\", \"United\", \"Whites\", \"Al Nahdi\", \"Lemon\"]\n\ncustomers = set(orders_jan)\nprint(len(customers))                 # 4\nprint(sorted(customers))              # ['Al Nahdi', 'Lemon', 'United', 'Whites']\nprint(\"Al Dawaa\" in customers)        # False\nprint(list(dict.fromkeys(orders_jan)))  # ['Al Nahdi', 'United', 'Whites', 'Lemon']\n\ncustomers.add(\"Al Dawaa\")\ncustomers.discard(\"Lemon\")\nprint(sorted(customers))              # ['Al Dawaa', 'Al Nahdi', 'United', 'Whites']\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- Sets store unique, hashable values without order.\n- `set()` (not `{}`) creates an empty set.\n- Use sets for deduplication and fast `in` checks.\n",
