    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Perform Venn diagram operations on sets to compare datasets with mathematical rigor.",
    "content": "# Lesson 40: Set Operations: Union (|), Intersection (&), Difference (-) and Symmetric Difference (^)\n\nSet operations answer business questions in one line: *Which customers ordered in both months? Who stopped ordering? Who is new?*\n\n## 1. The operations\n\n| Operation | Operator | Method | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Union | `a \\| b` | `a.union(b)` | Everything in a **or** b |\n| Intersection | `a & b` | `a.intersection(b)` | In **both** |\n| Difference | `a - b` | `a.difference(b)` | In a but **not** in b |\n| Symmetric difference | `a ^ b` | `a.symmetric_difference(b)` | In **one but not both** |\n\nComparisons: `a <= b` (a is a subset of b), `a >= b` (superset), `a.isdisjoint(b)` (no common items).\n\n## 2. Worked examples\n```python\noct_customers = {\"Al Nahdi\", \"United\", \"Whites\", \"Lemon\"}\nnov_customers = {\"Al Nahdi\", \"United\", \"Al Dawaa\", \"Boots\"}\n```\n\n### Example 1: Ordered in both months (loyal)\n```python\noct_customers = {\"Al Nahdi\", \"United\", \"Whites\", \"Lemon\"}\nnov_customers = {\"Al Nahdi\", \"United\", \"Al Dawaa\", \"Boots\"}\nprint(sorted(oct_customers & nov_customers))   # ['Al Nahdi', 'United']\n```\n\n### Example 2: New in November\n```python\noct_customers = {\"Al Nahdi\", \"United\", \"Whites\", \"Lemon\"}\nnov_customers = {\"Al Nahdi\", \"United\", \"Al Dawaa\", \"Boots\"}\nprint(sorted(nov_customers - oct_customers))   # ['Al Dawaa', 'Boots']\n```\n\n### Example 3: Lost customers (ordered in Oct, not Nov)\n```python\noct_customers = {\"Al Nahdi\", \"United\", \"Whites\", \"Lemon\"}\nnov_customers = {\"Al Nahdi\", \"United\", \"Al Dawaa\", \"Boots\"}\nprint(sorted(oct_customers - nov_customers))   # ['Lemon', 'Whites']\n```\n\n### Example 4: All customers and \"changed\" customers\n```python\noct_customers = {\"Al Nahdi\", \"United\", \"Whites\", \"Lemon\"}\nnov_customers = {\"Al Nahdi\", \"United\", \"Al Dawaa\", \"Boots\"}\nprint(len(oct_customers | nov_customers))      # 6 distinct customers\nprint(sorted(oct_customers ^ nov_customers))   # ['Al Dawaa', 'Boots', 'Lemon', 'Whites']\n```\n\n### Example 5: Required documents check\n```python\nrequired = {\"ID\", \"Contract\", \"License\"}\nreceived = {\"ID\", \"License\"}\nmissing = required - received\nprint(\"Missing:\", missing or \"nothing\")        # Missing: {'Contract'}\nprint(required <= received)                    # False - not all received\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> These are the same ideas as Venn diagrams - and as Left Anti / Inner joins in Power Query (Excel Lesson 99).\n\n## 3. Common mistakes\n- **Using `+`** on sets -> TypeError; use `|`.\n- **Order of difference matters**: `a - b` ≠ `b - a`.\n- **Comparing lists** instead of sets - convert first: `set(list1) & set(list2)`.\n\n## 4. Hands-on Student Challenge\n```python\ncourse_excel = [\"Ali\", \"Noura\", \"Faisal\", \"Reem\", \"Sara\"]\ncourse_python = [\"Noura\", \"Omar\", \"Sara\", \"Khalid\"]\n```\n1. Students in both courses.\n2. Students only in Excel.\n3. All students (count).\n4. Students in exactly one course.\n5. Is everyone in Python also in Excel?\n\n:::solution\n```python\nexcel = set([\"Ali\", \"Noura\", \"Faisal\", \"Reem\", \"Sara\"])\npython = set([\"Noura\", \"Omar\", \"Sara\", \"Khalid\"])\n\nprint(sorted(excel & python))      # ['Noura', 'Sara']\nprint(sorted(excel - python))      # ['Ali', 'Faisal', 'Reem']\nprint(len(excel | python))         # 7\nprint(sorted(excel ^ python))      # ['Ali', 'Faisal', 'Khalid', 'Omar', 'Reem']\nprint(python <= excel)             # False (Omar, Khalid only in Python)\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- `|` union, `&` intersection, `-` difference, `^` symmetric difference.\n- Use them to compare customer lists, attendance and requirements.\n- Convert lists to sets first.\n",
    "tags": "Python, Lesson 40, Programming, Intermediate",
