    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Accept arbitrary named options packaged into a dictionary.",
    "content": "# Lesson 45: Arbitrary Keyword Arguments: **kwargs for Dynamic Options\n\n`**kwargs` collects any number of **keyword arguments** into a **dictionary** - useful for optional settings, flexible records, and passing options through to other functions.\n\n## 1. Syntax\n```python\ndef create_customer(name, **details):\n    print(name, details)\n\ncreate_customer(\"Al Nahdi\", city=\"Jeddah\", phone=\"0501234567\", vip=True)\n# Al Nahdi {'city': 'Jeddah', 'phone': '0501234567', 'vip': True}\n```\n\n## 2. Full parameter order\n```text\ndef f(required, optional=default, *args, keyword_only=default, **kwargs)\n```\n\n## 3. Unpacking a dict into arguments\n```python\ndef invoice(customer, amount, currency=\"SAR\"):\n    return f\"{customer}: {amount:,} {currency}\"\n\ndata = {\"customer\": \"United\", \"amount\": 3100}\nprint(invoice(**data))           # United: 3,100 SAR\n```\n\n## 4. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Flexible records\n```python\ndef make_product(name, price, **extra):\n    product = {\"name\": name, \"price\": price}\n    product.update(extra)\n    return product\n\nprint(make_product(\"Gel\", 40, size=\"10ml\", category=\"Eye care\"))\n```\n\n### Example 2: Report options with defaults\n```python\ndef report(data, **options):\n    title = options.get(\"title\", \"Report\")\n    decimals = options.get(\"decimals\", 0)\n    total = round(sum(data), decimals)\n    return f\"{title}: {total:,}\"\n\nprint(report([1200.456, 300.2]))\nprint(report([1200.456, 300.2], title=\"Q4 Sales\", decimals=2))\n```\n\n### Example 3: Passing options through\n```python\ndef format_amount(value, currency=\"SAR\", decimals=2):\n    return f\"{value:,.{decimals}f} {currency}\"\n\ndef print_line(label, value, **fmt):\n    print(f\"{label:<10}\", format_amount(value, **fmt))\n\nprint_line(\"Total\", 1234.5)\nprint_line(\"Total\", 1234.5, currency=\"USD\", decimals=0)\n```\n\n### Example 4: Merging dictionaries\n```python\ndefaults = {\"currency\": \"SAR\", \"vat\": 0.15}\ncustom = {\"vat\": 0.05}\nsettings = {**defaults, **custom}\nprint(settings)    # {'currency': 'SAR', 'vat': 0.05}\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Validate unexpected options: `unknown = set(kwargs) - {\"title\", \"decimals\"}` and raise an error if it's not empty - this catches typos like `titel=\"...\"`.\n\n## 5. Common mistakes\n- **Typos in option names** silently ignored.\n- **Passing a dict without `**`** - it becomes one positional argument.\n- **Overusing kwargs** - explicit parameters are easier to understand.\n\n## 6. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Write `build_email(to, subject, **fields)` that returns a dict with `to`, `subject`, and any extra fields (cc, body, priority...).\n2. Write `describe(**info)` that prints each key and value on its own line, sorted by key.\n3. Use dict unpacking to merge default email settings `{\"priority\": \"normal\", \"cc\": \"\"}` with custom ones.\n\n:::solution\n```python\ndef build_email(to, subject, **fields):\n    email = {\"to\": to, \"subject\": subject}\n    email.update(fields)\n    return email\n\ndef describe(**info):\n    for key in sorted(info):\n        print(f\"{key}: {info[key]}\")\n\ne = build_email(\"sara@company.com\", \"Price list\", cc=\"manager@company.com\", priority=\"high\")\nprint(e)\ndescribe(**e)\n\ndefaults = {\"priority\": \"normal\", \"cc\": \"\"}\ncustom = {\"priority\": \"high\"}\nprint({**defaults, **custom})    # {'priority': 'high', 'cc': ''}\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- `**kwargs` collects keyword arguments into a dict.\n- `**dict` spreads a dict into keyword arguments.\n- Great for optional settings - but validate names to catch typos.\n",
    "tags": "Python, Lesson 45, Programming, Intermediate",
    "read_time": 4,
    "author": "Mohammed Bargisy",
    "status": "published",
