    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Construct optimized dictionaries and unique sets with comprehension syntax.",
    "content": "# Lesson 55: Dictionary & Set Comprehensions\n\nThe comprehension syntax also builds **dictionaries** and **sets** - lookup tables and unique collections in one line.\n\n## 1. Dictionary comprehension\n```text\n{key_expr: value_expr for item in iterable if condition}\n```\n```python\nproducts = [\"Gel\", \"Drops\", \"Wipes\"]\nprices = [40, 32, 18]\nprice_list = {p: price for p, price in zip(products, prices)}\nprint(price_list)     # {'Gel': 40, 'Drops': 32, 'Wipes': 18}\n```\n\n## 2. Set comprehension\n```text\n{expression for item in iterable}\n```\n```python\nvisits = [\"Al Nahdi\", \"united\", \"AL NAHDI\", \"Whites\"]\nprint({v.title() for v in visits})     # {'Al Nahdi', 'United', 'Whites'}\n```\n\n## 3. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Transform dictionary values\n```python\nprices = {\"Gel\": 40, \"Drops\": 32, \"Wipes\": 18}\nwith_vat = {p: round(v * 1.15, 2) for p, v in prices.items()}\nprint(with_vat)\n```\n\n### Example 2: Filter a dictionary\n```python\nstock = {\"Gel\": 120, \"Drops\": 0, \"Wipes\": 300, \"Spray\": 8}\nlow = {p: q for p, q in stock.items() if q < 20}\nprint(low)       # {'Drops': 0, 'Spray': 8}\n```\n\n### Example 3: Swap keys and values\n```python\ncodes = {\"JED\": \"Jeddah\", \"MAK\": \"Makkah\"}\nnames = {v: k for k, v in codes.items()}\nprint(names)     # {'Jeddah': 'JED', 'Makkah': 'MAK'}\n```\n\n### Example 4: Word lengths\n```python\nwords = [\"gel\", \"drops\", \"wipes\"]\nprint({w: len(w) for w in words})\n```\n\n### Example 5: Unique domains\n```python\nemails = [\"a@company.com\", \"b@company.com\", \"c@gmail.com\"]\nprint({e.split(\"@\")[1] for e in emails})    # {'company.com', 'gmail.com'}\n```\n\n### Example 6: Achievement per rep\n```python\ntargets = {\"Ahmed\": 12000, \"Sara\": 10000}\nactual = {\"Ahmed\": 14000, \"Sara\": 9000}\nachievement = {rep: f\"{actual[rep] / t:.0%}\" for rep, t in targets.items()}\nprint(achievement)     # {'Ahmed': '117%', 'Sara': '90%'}\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> `{}` with `key: value` makes a dict; `{}` with single values makes a set. An **empty** `{}` is always a dict.\n\n## 4. Common mistakes\n- **Duplicate keys** - later values overwrite earlier ones silently.\n- **Expecting order in a set.**\n- **Using a set comprehension where you need counts** - use a dict or Counter.\n\n## 5. Hands-on Student Challenge\n```python\nsales = [(\"Jeddah\", 14000), (\"Makkah\", 9000), (\"Jeddah\", 16000), (\"Taif\", 4500)]\n```\n1. Set of regions.\n2. Dict region -> total sales (hint: use the set from step 1 and `sum` inside the comprehension).\n3. Dict of regions whose total is above 10,000.\n4. Dict region -> region code (first 3 letters uppercase).\n\n:::solution\n```python\nsales = [(\"Jeddah\", 14000), (\"Makkah\", 9000), (\"Jeddah\", 16000), (\"Taif\", 4500)]\n\nregions = {r for r, _ in sales}\nprint(regions)                                   # {'Jeddah', 'Makkah', 'Taif'}\n\ntotals = {reg: sum(a for r, a in sales if r == reg) for reg in regions}\nprint(totals)                                    # Jeddah 30000, Makkah 9000, Taif 4500\n\nprint({r: t for r, t in totals.items() if t > 10000})   # {'Jeddah': 30000}\nprint({r: r[:3].upper() for r in sorted(regions)})      # {'Jeddah': 'JED', 'Makkah': 'MAK', 'Taif': 'TAI'}\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- `{k: v for ...}` builds dicts; `{x for ...}` builds sets.\n- Transform, filter and invert dictionaries in one line.\n- An empty `{}` is a dict - use `set()` for an empty set.\n",
