    "id": "py-57",
    "lesson_number": 57,
    "title": "Lesson 57: The random Module: Seeds, randint(), choice(), and shuffle()",
    "slug": "python-lesson-57-the-random-module-seeds-randint-choice-and-shuffle",
    "category": "programming",
    "track": "python",
    "track_name": "Python Path",
    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Generate pseudorandom numbers, select lottery winners, and shuffle decks.",
    "content": "# Lesson 57: The random Module: Seeds, randint(), choice(), and shuffle()\n\nThe `random` module generates random numbers and choices - for games, sampling, test data and simulations.\n\n## 1. Key functions\n\n| Function | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `random.random()` | Float from 0.0 to <1.0 |\n| `random.randint(a, b)` | Integer from a to b (**both included**) |\n| `random.uniform(a, b)` | Float between a and b |\n| `random.choice(seq)` | One random item |\n| `random.choices(seq, k=n)` | n items **with** replacement (repeats possible) |\n| `random.sample(seq, k=n)` | n **unique** items |\n| `random.shuffle(lst)` | Shuffles a list **in place** |\n| `random.seed(n)` | Makes results repeatable |\n\n## 2. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Dice and coins\n```python\nimport random\nprint(random.randint(1, 6))\nprint(random.choice([\"Heads\", \"Tails\"]))\n```\n\n### Example 2: Repeatable results with a seed\n```python\nimport random\nrandom.seed(42)\nprint([random.randint(1, 100) for _ in range(5)])\nrandom.seed(42)\nprint([random.randint(1, 100) for _ in range(5)])   # identical list\n```\nSeeds are essential for tests and reproducible simulations.\n\n### Example 3: Pick visit winners\n```python\nimport random\ncustomers = [\"Al Nahdi\", \"Al Dawaa\", \"United\", \"Whites\", \"Lemon\"]\nprint(random.sample(customers, k=2))     # 2 different customers\n```\n\n### Example 4: Shuffle quiz questions\n```python\nimport random\nquestions = [\"Q1\", \"Q2\", \"Q3\", \"Q4\"]\nrandom.shuffle(questions)\nprint(questions)\n```\n\n### Example 5: Generate test sales data\n```python\nimport random\nrandom.seed(1)\nregions = [\"Jeddah\", \"Makkah\", \"Taif\"]\ndata = [(random.choice(regions), random.randint(500, 5000)) for _ in range(5)]\nfor row in data:\n    print(row)\n```\n\n### Example 6: Weighted choice\n```python\nimport random\noutcome = random.choices([\"Order\", \"No order\"], weights=[70, 30], k=10)\nprint(outcome.count(\"Order\"), \"orders out of 10 visits\")\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> For passwords and security codes, use the `secrets` module (Lesson 60), not `random` - `random` is predictable.\n\n## 3. Common mistakes\n- **`x = random.shuffle(lst)`** -> x is None (it shuffles in place).\n- **randint end included** - `randint(1, 6)` can return 6 (unlike `range`).\n- **sample with k larger than the list** -> ValueError.\n\n## 4. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Simulate rolling two dice 1,000 times and count how often the total is 7 (use seed 7).\n2. Randomly split 10 employees into two teams of 5.\n3. Generate 5 random 6-digit order codes like `ORD-483920`.\n\n:::solution\n```python\nimport random\n\nrandom.seed(7)\nsevens = sum(1 for _ in range(1000) if random.randint(1, 6) + random.randint(1, 6) == 7)\nprint(\"Sevens:\", sevens, f\"({sevens / 1000:.1%})\")   # about 1/6 ≈ 16.7%\n\nemployees = [f\"Emp{i}\" for i in range(1, 11)]\nrandom.shuffle(employees)\nteam_a, team_b = employees[:5], employees[5:]\nprint(team_a, team_b)\n\nprint([f\"ORD-{random.randint(0, 999999):06d}\" for _ in range(5)])\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- randint, choice, sample, shuffle cover most needs.\n- `random.seed()` makes results repeatable.\n- Use `secrets` for anything security-related.\n",
    "tags": "Python, Lesson 57, Programming, Intermediate",
