    "id": "py-58",
    "lesson_number": 58,
    "title": "Lesson 58: The datetime Module: Working with Dates, Times, and Timestamps",
    "slug": "python-lesson-58-the-datetime-module-working-with-dates-times-and-timestamps",
    "category": "programming",
    "track": "python",
    "track_name": "Python Path",
    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Handle calendar dates, timestamps, and compute elapsed durations.",
    "content": "# Lesson 58: The datetime Module: Working with Dates, Times, and Timestamps\n\nThe `datetime` module handles dates and times: today's date, differences between dates, due dates, ages and timestamps.\n\n## 1. The main classes\n```python\nfrom datetime import date, datetime, timedelta\n\ntoday = date(2026, 11, 17)            # a specific date\nnow = datetime(2026, 11, 17, 9, 30)   # date + time\nprint(today, now)\nprint(today.year, today.month, today.day)\nprint(today.weekday())                # Monday=0 ... Sunday=6\n```\nFor the real current date use `date.today()` and `datetime.now()`.\n\n## 2. timedelta - durations\n```python\nfrom datetime import date, timedelta\nd = date(2026, 11, 17)\nprint(d + timedelta(days=30))         # 2026-12-17\nprint(d - timedelta(weeks=2))         # 2026-11-03\n```\n\n## 3. Differences between dates\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\ndue = date(2026, 12, 31)\ntoday = date(2026, 11, 17)\ndelta = due - today\nprint(delta.days, \"days left\")        # 44 days left\n```\n\n## 4. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Invoice due date\n```python\nfrom datetime import date, timedelta\ninvoice_date = date(2026, 11, 17)\ndue = invoice_date + timedelta(days=30)\nprint(f\"Due: {due}\")\n```\n\n### Example 2: Overdue check\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\ndue_dates = [date(2026, 11, 1), date(2026, 11, 30)]\ntoday = date(2026, 11, 17)\nfor d in due_dates:\n    days = (today - d).days\n    print(d, \"overdue by\", days, \"days\" if days > 0 else \"- not due yet\")\n```\n\n### Example 3: Age in years\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\nbirth = date(1985, 3, 25)\ntoday = date(2026, 11, 17)\nage = today.year - birth.year - ((today.month, today.day) < (birth.month, birth.day))\nprint(age)    # 41\n```\n\n### Example 4: Hours worked\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime\nstart = datetime(2026, 11, 17, 8, 30)\nend = datetime(2026, 11, 17, 17, 15)\nprint((end - start).total_seconds() / 3600)   # 8.75\n```\n\n### Example 5: First and last day of the month\n```python\nfrom datetime import date, timedelta\nd = date(2026, 11, 17)\nfirst = d.replace(day=1)\nnext_month = (first + timedelta(days=32)).replace(day=1)\nlast = next_month - timedelta(days=1)\nprint(first, last)     # 2026-11-01 2026-11-30\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Store and exchange dates in ISO format `YYYY-MM-DD` (`d.isoformat()`) - it sorts correctly and every system understands it.\n\n## 5. Common mistakes\n- **Adding months with timedelta** - timedelta has no months (months have different lengths).\n- **Mixing date and datetime** in subtraction -> TypeError.\n- **Using `.seconds`** instead of `.total_seconds()` (seconds ignores days).\n\n## 6. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Contract signed 2026-11-17 for 90 days - what is the end date, and what weekday is it?\n2. How many days from 2026-11-17 to 2027-01-01?\n3. A shift runs from 22:00 on 17 Nov to 06:30 on 18 Nov - how many hours?\n4. List the dates of the next 5 Sundays from 2026-11-17.\n\n:::solution\n```python\nfrom datetime import date, datetime, timedelta\n\nend = date(2026, 11, 17) + timedelta(days=90)\nprint(end, end.strftime(\"%A\"))                   # 2027-02-15 Monday\n\nprint((date(2027, 1, 1) - date(2026, 11, 17)).days)   # 45\n\nshift = datetime(2026, 11, 18, 6, 30) - datetime(2026, 11, 17, 22, 0)\nprint(shift.total_seconds() / 3600)              # 8.5\n\nd = date(2026, 11, 17)\ndays_to_sunday = (6 - d.weekday()) % 7 or 7      # Sunday = 6\nfirst_sunday = d + timedelta(days=days_to_sunday)\nprint([str(first_sunday + timedelta(weeks=i)) for i in range(5)])\n# ['2026-11-22', '2026-11-29', '2026-12-06', '2026-12-13', '2026-12-20']\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- `date`, `datetime`, `timedelta` handle dates, times and durations.\n- Subtracting dates gives a timedelta (`.days`, `.total_seconds()`).\n- Use ISO format for storing dates.\n",
    "tags": "Python, Lesson 58, Programming, Intermediate",
