    "summary": "Combine strings, random choices, conditionals, and functions to create strong passwords.",
    "content": "# Lesson 60: Mini-Project 3: Secure Password Generator with Entropy Scorer\n\nThis project combines strings, loops, functions, `random`/`secrets` and `math` to build a **password generator** that also scores password strength using **entropy**.\n\n## 1. Why `secrets`?\n`random` is predictable (it's designed for simulations). For passwords, use `secrets`, which uses the operating system's secure random source.\n```python\nimport secrets, string\nprint(secrets.choice(string.ascii_letters))\nprint(secrets.token_hex(8))       # e.g. '9f86d081884c7d65'\n```\n\n## 2. Building the generator\n```python\nimport secrets\nimport string\n\ndef generate_password(length=14, use_symbols=True):\n    pool = string.ascii_letters + string.digits\n    if use_symbols:\n        pool += \"!@#$%^&*-_?\"\n    return \"\".join(secrets.choice(pool) for _ in range(length))\n\nprint(generate_password())\nprint(generate_password(20, use_symbols=False))\n```\n\n### Guarantee one of each type\n```python\nimport secrets, string\n\ndef strong_password(length=14):\n    groups = [string.ascii_lowercase, string.ascii_uppercase, string.digits, \"!@#$%^&*-_?\"]\n    chars = [secrets.choice(g) for g in groups]               # one from each group\n    pool = \"\".join(groups)\n    chars += [secrets.choice(pool) for _ in range(length - len(chars))]\n    secrets.SystemRandom().shuffle(chars)\n    return \"\".join(chars)\n\nprint(strong_password())\n```\n\n## 3. Entropy - measuring strength\nEntropy (in bits) estimates how hard a password is to guess: `length x log2(pool size)`.\n\n| Bits | Rough strength |\n|---|---|\n| < 40 | Weak |\n| 40-60 | Fair |\n| 60-80 | Strong |\n| > 80 | Very strong |\n\n```python\nimport math, string\n\ndef entropy_bits(password):\n    pool = 0\n    if any(c.islower() for c in password): pool += 26\n    if any(c.isupper() for c in password): pool += 26\n    if any(c.isdigit() for c in password): pool += 10\n    if any(not c.isalnum() for c in password): pool += 32\n    return len(password) * math.log2(pool) if pool else 0\n\nfor pw in [\"password\", \"Jeddah2026\", \"vR7#qL2!mZ9@xT4k\"]:\n    print(f\"{pw:<18} {entropy_bits(pw):5.1f} bits\")\n```\n(Real attackers use dictionaries, so `password` is even weaker than its entropy suggests.)\n\n## 4. Passphrases\n```python\nimport secrets\nwords = [\"camel\", \"window\", \"orange\", \"piano\", \"falcon\", \"river\", \"lantern\", \"mango\", \"desert\", \"cloud\"]\nphrase = \"-\".join(secrets.choice(words) for _ in range(4)) + \"-\" + str(secrets.randbelow(100))\nprint(phrase)\n```\n(A real word list has thousands of words, giving much higher entropy.)\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Never print or log real passwords in production systems - this project is for learning.\n\n## 5. Common mistakes\n- **Using `random` for passwords.**\n- **Short passwords with symbols** - length matters more than complexity.\n- **Not guaranteeing character types** when a system requires them.\n\n## 6. Hands-on Student Challenge\nBuild a complete tool:\n1. `strong_password(length)` that guarantees all four character types.\n2. `rate(password)` returning (bits, label) using the table above.\n3. Generate 5 passwords of length 12 and print each with its rating.\n4. Also rate three passwords typed by you (use a list, not input).\n\n:::solution\n```python\nimport math, secrets, string\n\nSYMBOLS = \"!@#$%^&*-_?\"\n\ndef strong_password(length=12):\n    groups = [string.ascii_lowercase, string.ascii_uppercase, string.digits, SYMBOLS]\n    chars = [secrets.choice(g) for g in groups]\n    pool = \"\".join(groups)\n    chars += [secrets.choice(pool) for _ in range(length - 4)]\n    secrets.SystemRandom().shuffle(chars)\n    return \"\".join(chars)\n\ndef rate(password):\n    pool = 0\n    if any(c.islower() for c in password): pool += 26\n    if any(c.isupper() for c in password): pool += 26\n    if any(c.isdigit() for c in password): pool += 10\n    if any(not c.isalnum() for c in password): pool += 32\n    bits = len(password) * math.log2(pool) if pool else 0\n    if bits < 40: label = \"Weak\"\n    elif bits < 60: label = \"Fair\"\n    elif bits < 80: label = \"Strong\"\n    else: label = \"Very strong\"\n    return round(bits, 1), label\n\nfor _ in range(5):\n    pw = strong_password(12)\n    print(pw, rate(pw))          # 12 chars, pool 94 -> 78.7 bits, Strong\n\nfor pw in [\"qwerty\", \"Sara1990\", \"Lantern-Falcon-Mango-82\"]:\n    print(pw, rate(pw))\n# qwerty (28.2, 'Weak') / Sara1990 (47.6, 'Fair') / Lantern-Falcon-Mango-82 (150.8, 'Very strong')\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- Use `secrets` for passwords and tokens.\n- Guarantee required character types, then shuffle.\n- Entropy (bits) = length x log2(pool) - length is the biggest factor.\n",
