    "id": "py-65",
    "lesson_number": 65,
    "title": "Lesson 65: Reading Text Files: open(), read(), readline(), and readlines()",
    "slug": "python-lesson-65-reading-text-files-open-read-readline-and-readlines",
    "category": "programming",
    "track": "python",
    "track_name": "Python Path",
    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Inspect and read contents from text files using Python's built-in file primitives.",
    "content": "# Lesson 65: Reading Text Files: open(), read(), readline(), and readlines()\n\nPrograms often need to read data from **text files** - logs, exports, configuration. Python's `open()` function opens a file; then you read it in different ways.\n\n> In the sandbox, files live in a temporary virtual folder that exists while the page is open. The examples first create a small file so you can read it.\n\n## 1. Opening and reading\n```python\n# Create a sample file first\nf = open(\"customers.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\nf.write(\"Al Nahdi\\nAl Dawaa\\nUnited\\nWhites\\n\")\nf.close()\n\nf = open(\"customers.txt\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\ncontent = f.read()          # the whole file as one string\nf.close()\nprint(content)\n```\nAlways `close()` the file - or better, use `with` (Lesson 66).\n\n## 2. Reading methods\n\n| Method | Returns |\n|---|---|\n| `f.read()` | Whole file as one string |\n| `f.readline()` | The next single line (including `\\n`) |\n| `f.readlines()` | List of all lines |\n| `for line in f:` | Loops line by line (memory-efficient) |\n\n## 3. Worked examples\n### Example 1: readline\n```python\nwith open(\"names.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    f.write(\"Ahmed\\nSara\\nOmar\\n\")\n\nf = open(\"names.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\nprint(repr(f.readline()))   # 'Ahmed\\n'\nprint(repr(f.readline()))   # 'Sara\\n'\nf.close()\n```\n\n### Example 2: readlines and strip\n```python\nwith open(\"names.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    f.write(\"Ahmed\\nSara\\nOmar\\n\")\n\nf = open(\"names.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\nlines = [line.strip() for line in f.readlines()]\nf.close()\nprint(lines)      # ['Ahmed', 'Sara', 'Omar']\n```\n\n### Example 3: Loop line by line and count\n```python\nwith open(\"log.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    f.write(\"INFO start\\nERROR db timeout\\nINFO retry\\nERROR db timeout\\nINFO done\\n\")\n\nerrors = 0\nf = open(\"log.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\nfor line in f:\n    if line.startswith(\"ERROR\"):\n        errors += 1\nf.close()\nprint(\"Errors:\", errors)   # 2\n```\n\n### Example 4: Missing files\n```python\ntry:\n    f = open(\"missing.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\nexcept FileNotFoundError:\n    print(\"File not found - check the name and folder\")\n```\n\n### Example 5: Numbers from a file\n```python\nwith open(\"sales.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    f.write(\"14000\\n9000\\n16000\\n\")\n\nf = open(\"sales.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\nvalues = [int(line) for line in f if line.strip()]\nf.close()\nprint(sum(values))   # 39000\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Always pass `encoding=\"utf-8\"` - it makes Arabic and other non-English text work reliably on every computer.\n\n## 4. Common mistakes\n- **Forgetting `\\n` at the end of lines** - use `.strip()`.\n- **Reading a huge file with `read()`** - loop line by line instead.\n- **Wrong path or file name** -> FileNotFoundError.\n\n## 5. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Create `orders.txt` with 6 lines in the format `INV-101,Al Nahdi,4500`.\n2. Read it and print each customer and amount.\n3. Calculate the total amount.\n4. Print the line count and the largest order.\n\n:::solution\n```python\nwith open(\"orders.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    f.write(\"INV-101,Al Nahdi,4500\\nINV-102,Al Dawaa,1200\\nINV-103,United,8900\\n\"\n            \"INV-104,Whites,650\\nINV-105,Al Nahdi,2300\\nINV-106,Lemon,3100\\n\")\n\norders = []\nf = open(\"orders.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\nfor line in f:\n    inv, customer, amount = line.strip().split(\",\")\n    orders.append((inv, customer, int(amount)))\n    print(customer, amount)\nf.close()\n\nprint(\"Total:\", sum(o[2] for o in orders))          # 20650\nprint(\"Lines:\", len(orders))                         # 6\nprint(\"Largest:\", max(orders, key=lambda o: o[2]))   # ('INV-103', 'United', 8900)\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- `open(path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\")` opens a file for reading.\n- `read`, `readline`, `readlines`, or loop line by line.\n- Strip newlines and handle FileNotFoundError.\n",
    "tags": "Python, Lesson 65, Programming, Intermediate",
