    "id": "py-67",
    "lesson_number": 67,
    "title": "Lesson 67: Writing and Appending to Files ('w', 'a', 'r+' modes)",
    "slug": "python-lesson-67-writing-and-appending-to-files-w-a-r-modes",
    "category": "programming",
    "track": "python",
    "track_name": "Python Path",
    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Distinguish destructive write ('w') from cumulative append ('a') modes.",
    "content": "# Lesson 67: Writing and Appending to Files ('w', 'a', 'r+' modes)\n\nWriting files lets your programs save results: reports, logs, cleaned data. The **mode** decides whether you overwrite, add to, or update a file.\n\n## 1. File modes\n\n| Mode | Meaning | File exists | File missing |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `\"r\"` | Read (default) | Read from start | Error |\n| `\"w\"` | Write | **Erases** content | Creates |\n| `\"a\"` | Append | Adds at the end | Creates |\n| `\"x\"` | Exclusive create | **Error** | Creates |\n| `\"r+\"` | Read and write | From start, no erase | Error |\n\nAdd `\"b\"` for binary files (`\"rb\"`, `\"wb\"`) such as images.\n\n## 2. Writing\n```python\nwith open(\"report.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    f.write(\"Sales Report\\n\")\n    f.write(\"Total: 39,000 SAR\\n\")\n\nlines = [\"Ahmed: 14000\\n\", \"Sara: 9000\\n\"]\nwith open(\"report.txt\", \"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    f.writelines(lines)\n\nwith open(\"report.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    print(f.read())\n```\n`write()` does **not** add a new line - include `\\n` yourself.\n\n## 3. Worked examples\n### Example 1: A simple log\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\ndef log(message):\n    with open(\"app.log\", \"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        f.write(f\"{datetime(2026, 11, 17, 9, 0):%Y-%m-%d %H:%M} {message}\\n\")\n\nlog(\"Report started\")\nlog(\"Report finished\")\nwith open(\"app.log\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    print(f.read())\n```\n\n### Example 2: print() into a file\n```python\nwith open(\"summary.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    print(\"Region\", \"Sales\", sep=\"\\t\", file=f)\n    print(\"Jeddah\", 30000, sep=\"\\t\", file=f)\n\nwith open(\"summary.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    print(f.read())\n```\n\n### Example 3: Don't overwrite by accident\n```python\nwith open(\"important.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    f.write(\"v1\")\ntry:\n    with open(\"important.txt\", \"x\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        f.write(\"v2\")\nexcept FileExistsError:\n    print(\"File already exists - not overwritten\")\n```\n\n### Example 4: Update a value with r+\n```python\nwith open(\"counter.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    f.write(\"41\")\nwith open(\"counter.txt\", \"r+\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    n = int(f.read())\n    f.seek(0)            # go back to the start\n    f.write(str(n + 1))\n    f.truncate()         # remove leftover characters\nwith open(\"counter.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    print(f.read())      # 42\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Mode `\"w\"` erases the file **the moment it opens**, even if you write nothing. Double-check before using it on important files.\n\n## 4. Common mistakes\n- **Using \"w\" when you meant \"a\"** - previous content is lost.\n- **Writing numbers directly**: `f.write(5)` -> TypeError; use `str(5)`.\n- **Forgetting `\\n`** - everything ends up on one line.\n\n## 5. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Write a function `save_order(inv, customer, amount)` that appends a line `inv,customer,amount` to `orders.csv` (create a header line if the file doesn't exist yet).\n2. Save 3 orders.\n3. Read the file and print the total amount.\n4. Write a summary file `summary.txt` (overwrite mode) containing the count and total.\n\n:::solution\n```python\nimport os\n\ndef save_order(inv, customer, amount, path=\"orders.csv\"):\n    new_file = not os.path.exists(path)\n    with open(path, \"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        if new_file:\n            f.write(\"invoice,customer,amount\\n\")\n        f.write(f\"{inv},{customer},{amount}\\n\")\n\nif os.path.exists(\"orders.csv\"):\n    os.remove(\"orders.csv\")           # start fresh for this exercise\n\nsave_order(\"INV-101\", \"Al Nahdi\", 4500)\nsave_order(\"INV-102\", \"Al Dawaa\", 1200)\nsave_order(\"INV-103\", \"United\", 8900)\n\nwith open(\"orders.csv\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    next(f)                           # skip header\n    amounts = [int(line.strip().split(\",\")[2]) for line in f]\n\nwith open(\"summary.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    f.write(f\"Orders: {len(amounts)}\\nTotal: {sum(amounts):,} SAR\\n\")\n\nwith open(\"summary.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    print(f.read())\n# Orders: 3\n# Total: 14,600 SAR\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- \"w\" overwrites, \"a\" appends, \"x\" refuses to overwrite, \"r+\" updates.\n- `write()` needs strings and explicit `\\n`.\n- `print(..., file=f)` is a handy way to write formatted lines.\n",
    "tags": "Python, Lesson 67, Programming, Intermediate",
    "read_time": 4,
    "author": "Mohammed Bargisy",
    "status": "published",
    "views": 219,
    "cover_gradient": "linear-gradient(135deg, #1e40af, #6366f1)",
    "updated_at": "2026-11-17 09:00:00"
