    "id": "py-75",
    "lesson_number": 75,
    "title": "Lesson 75: Regex Substitution with re.sub() for Data Sanitization",
    "slug": "python-lesson-75-regex-substitution-with-re-sub-for-data-sanitization",
    "category": "programming",
    "track": "python",
    "track_name": "Python Path",
    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Advanced",
    "summary": "Clean messy documents, remove sensitive tokens, and reformat dates using regex replacements.",
    "content": "# Lesson 75: Regex Substitution with re.sub() for Data Sanitization\n\n`re.sub()` **replaces** text that matches a pattern - perfect for cleaning messy data: removing symbols from phone numbers, normalising spaces, masking sensitive information.\n\n## 1. Syntax\n```text\nre.sub(pattern, replacement, text, count=0, flags=0)\n```\n```python\nimport re\nprint(re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \" \", \"Al    Nahdi   Jeddah\"))     # Al Nahdi Jeddah\n```\n\n## 2. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Clean phone numbers\n```python\nimport re\nphones = [\"050-123-4567\", \"(055) 987 6543\", \"+966 50 111 2222\"]\nfor p in phones:\n    digits = re.sub(r\"\\D\", \"\", p)          # \\D = anything that is NOT a digit\n    if digits.startswith(\"966\"):\n        digits = \"0\" + digits[3:]\n    print(digits)\n# 0501234567 / 0559876543 / 0501112222\n```\n\n### Example 2: Remove currency text and separators\n```python\nimport re\nraw = [\"SAR 1,250.50\", \"950 SAR\", \"15,000.00 ريال\"]\nprint([float(re.sub(r\"[^\\d.]\", \"\", r)) for r in raw])   # [1250.5, 950.0, 15000.0]\n```\n\n### Example 3: Mask sensitive data\n```python\nimport re\ntext = \"Card 4111111111111111 used by 0501234567\"\nmasked = re.sub(r\"\\d(?=\\d{4})\", \"*\", text)     # replace digits that have 4 more digits after them\nprint(masked)    # Card ************1111 used by ******4567\n```\n\n### Example 4: Reorder with groups\nConvert `DD/MM/YYYY` to `YYYY-MM-DD`:\n```python\nimport re\ntext = \"Due 17/11/2026, paid 05/12/2026\"\nprint(re.sub(r\"(\\d{2})/(\\d{2})/(\\d{4})\", r\"\\3-\\2-\\1\", text))\n# Due 2026-11-17, paid 2026-12-05\n```\n\n### Example 5: A function as the replacement\n```python\nimport re\nprices = \"Gel 40, Drops 32, Wipes 18\"\nwith_vat = re.sub(r\"\\d+\", lambda m: f\"{int(m.group()) * 1.15:.2f}\", prices)\nprint(with_vat)    # Gel 46.00, Drops 36.80, Wipes 20.70\n```\n\n### Example 6: Remove extra whitespace and punctuation\n```python\nimport re\nname = \"  al-nahdi!!!   pharmacy ** \"\nclean = re.sub(r\"[^\\w\\s-]\", \"\", name)\nclean = re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \" \", clean).strip().title()\nprint(clean)     # Al-Nahdi Pharmacy\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> If you use the same pattern many times, compile it once: `PHONE = re.compile(r\"\\D\")` then `PHONE.sub(\"\", text)`.\n\n## 3. Common mistakes\n- **Replacement backreferences without a raw string** - use `r\"\\1\"`.\n- **Greedy `.*`** matching too much - use `.*?` for the shortest match.\n- **Removing characters you need** (like the decimal point).\n\n## 4. Hands-on Student Challenge\n```python\nrecords = [\n    \"  ahmed   ali , 050-123 4567 , 17/11/2026 \",\n    \"SARA  MOHAMMED,(055)9876543,05/12/2026\",\n    \"omar khaled , +966 53 333 4444 , 01/01/2027\",\n]\n```\nClean each record into `Name | 05XXXXXXXX | YYYY-MM-DD`:\n- Name: single spaces, Title case.\n- Phone: digits only, convert +966 to 0.\n- Date: convert to ISO with re.sub.\n\n:::solution\n```python\nimport re\n\nrecords = [\n    \"  ahmed   ali , 050-123 4567 , 17/11/2026 \",\n    \"SARA  MOHAMMED,(055)9876543,05/12/2026\",\n    \"omar khaled , +966 53 333 4444 , 01/01/2027\",\n]\n\nfor r in records:\n    name, phone, date = [part.strip() for part in r.split(\",\")]\n    name = re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \" \", name).title()\n    phone = re.sub(r\"\\D\", \"\", phone)\n    if phone.startswith(\"966\"):\n        phone = \"0\" + phone[3:]\n    date = re.sub(r\"(\\d{2})/(\\d{2})/(\\d{4})\", r\"\\3-\\2-\\1\", date)\n    print(f\"{name} | {phone} | {date}\")\n```\nOutput:\n```text\nAhmed Ali | 0501234567 | 2026-11-17\nSara Mohammed | 0559876543 | 2026-12-05\nOmar Khaled | 0533334444 | 2027-01-01\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- `re.sub(pattern, replacement, text)` replaces matches.\n- `\\D` (non-digits) and `[^...]` (not these characters) are great for cleaning.\n- Groups `\\1 \\2` reorder parts; a function can compute replacements.\n",
    "tags": "Python, Lesson 75, Programming, Advanced",
    "read_time": 4,
    "author": "Mohammed Bargisy",
    "status": "published",
    "views": 195,
    "cover_gradient": "linear-gradient(135deg, #312e81, #8b5cf6)",
    "updated_at": "2026-11-17 09:00:00"
