    "id": "py-94",
    "lesson_number": 94,
    "title": "Lesson 94: The functools Module: lru_cache for Memoization & Optimization",
    "slug": "python-lesson-94-the-functools-module-lru-cache-for-memoization-optimization",
    "category": "programming",
    "track": "python",
    "track_name": "Python Path",
    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Advanced",
    "summary": "Cache expensive function return values automatically to achieve 100x speedups.",
    "content": "# Lesson 94: The functools Module: lru_cache for Memoization & Optimization\n\n`functools.lru_cache` **remembers** the results of a function. If it's called again with the same arguments, the stored answer is returned instantly - this is called **memoization**.\n\n## 1. The problem\n```python\ndef fib(n):\n    if n < 2:\n        return n\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\n\nprint(fib(25))    # works, but recalculates the same values thousands of times\n```\n`fib(30)` makes over 1.6 million calls.\n\n## 2. The solution\n```python\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=None)\ndef fib(n):\n    if n < 2:\n        return n\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\n\nprint(fib(100))             # instant: 354224848179261915075\nprint(fib.cache_info())     # hits, misses, size\n```\n- `maxsize=None` - unlimited cache.\n- `maxsize=128` (default) - keeps the 128 most recently used results (**LRU** = Least Recently Used are dropped).\n- Python 3.9+: `@functools.cache` is the same as `lru_cache(maxsize=None)`.\n\n## 3. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Caching an expensive lookup\n```python\nfrom functools import lru_cache\nimport time\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=256)\ndef exchange_rate(currency):\n    time.sleep(0.2)                 # pretend this calls a slow web service\n    return {\"USD\": 3.75, \"EUR\": 4.05}.get(currency, 1.0)\n\nstart = time.perf_counter()\nfor c in [\"USD\", \"EUR\", \"USD\", \"USD\", \"EUR\"]:\n    exchange_rate(c)\nprint(f\"{time.perf_counter() - start:.2f}s\")   # ~0.4s - only 2 real calls\nprint(exchange_rate.cache_info())\n```\n\n### Example 2: Clearing the cache\n```python\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache\ndef tax_rate(country):\n    return {\"SA\": 0.15, \"AE\": 0.05}.get(country, 0)\n\nprint(tax_rate(\"SA\"))\ntax_rate.cache_clear()          # when the source data changes\n```\n\n### Example 3: Counting ways (dynamic programming)\nHow many ways to make 100 SAR from 5, 10, 20 and 50 notes?\n```python\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\nNOTES = (5, 10, 20, 50)\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=None)\ndef ways(amount, i=0):\n    if amount == 0:\n        return 1\n    if amount < 0 or i == len(NOTES):\n        return 0\n    return ways(amount - NOTES[i], i) + ways(amount, i + 1)\n\nprint(ways(100))     # 49\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Only cache **pure** functions - same input always gives the same output, with no side effects. Never cache functions that depend on the current time or changing data (unless you clear the cache).\n\n## 4. Common mistakes\n- **Unhashable arguments** (lists, dicts) -> TypeError. Pass tuples instead.\n- **Caching functions with side effects** (printing, saving) - they won't run again.\n- **Unlimited caches** growing forever in long-running programs.\n\n## 5. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Write a recursive function `stairs(n)` - the number of ways to climb n stairs taking 1 or 2 steps - and time `stairs(30)` without and with `@lru_cache`.\n2. Print the cache info.\n3. Explain why caching `datetime.now()`-based functions would be wrong.\n\n:::solution\n```python\nimport time\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\ndef stairs_slow(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 1\n    return stairs_slow(n - 1) + stairs_slow(n - 2)\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=None)\ndef stairs(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 1\n    return stairs(n - 1) + stairs(n - 2)\n\nt = time.perf_counter(); print(stairs_slow(30)); print(f\"slow: {time.perf_counter() - t:.3f}s\")\nt = time.perf_counter(); print(stairs(30)); print(f\"cached: {time.perf_counter() - t:.6f}s\")\nprint(stairs.cache_info())   # CacheInfo(hits=28, misses=31, maxsize=None, currsize=31)\n```\n3. A cached function returns the **stored** result - a function based on the current time would keep returning the first time it was called, which is wrong.\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- `@lru_cache` / `@cache` store results for repeated calls.\n- Huge speedups for recursive and slow lookup functions.\n- Cache only pure functions with hashable arguments.\n",
    "tags": "Python, Lesson 94, Programming, Advanced",
    "read_time": 4,
