    "id": "py-95",
    "lesson_number": 95,
    "title": "Lesson 95: The collections Module: Counter, defaultdict, and namedtuple",
    "slug": "python-lesson-95-the-collections-module-counter-defaultdict-and-namedtuple",
    "category": "programming",
    "track": "python",
    "track_name": "Python Path",
    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Advanced",
    "summary": "Harness specialized container data types for cleaner, faster code.",
    "content": "# Lesson 95: The collections Module: Counter, defaultdict, and namedtuple\n\nThe `collections` module provides powerful ready-made containers that solve common tasks in one line: counting (`Counter`), grouping (`defaultdict`) and readable records (`namedtuple`).\n\n## 1. Counter - counting things\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\nregions = [\"Jeddah\", \"Makkah\", \"Jeddah\", \"Taif\", \"Jeddah\", \"Makkah\"]\ncounts = Counter(regions)\nprint(counts)                    # Counter({'Jeddah': 3, 'Makkah': 2, 'Taif': 1})\nprint(counts[\"Jeddah\"])          # 3\nprint(counts[\"Riyadh\"])          # 0 - no KeyError\nprint(counts.most_common(2))     # [('Jeddah', 3), ('Makkah', 2)]\n```\n\nCounters can be updated and added:\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\nweek1 = Counter({\"Gel\": 30, \"Drops\": 12})\nweek2 = Counter({\"Gel\": 25, \"Wipes\": 40})\nprint(week1 + week2)             # Counter({'Gel': 55, 'Wipes': 40, 'Drops': 12})\n```\n\n## 2. defaultdict - automatic default values\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\norders = [(\"Al Nahdi\", 4500), (\"United\", 3100), (\"Al Nahdi\", 2300)]\nby_customer = defaultdict(list)\nfor customer, amount in orders:\n    by_customer[customer].append(amount)      # no need to create the list first\nprint(dict(by_customer))   # {'Al Nahdi': [4500, 2300], 'United': [3100]}\n\ntotals = defaultdict(int)\nfor customer, amount in orders:\n    totals[customer] += amount\nprint(dict(totals))        # {'Al Nahdi': 6800, 'United': 3100}\n```\n\n## 3. namedtuple - records with field names\n```python\nfrom collections import namedtuple\nSale = namedtuple(\"Sale\", \"rep region amount\")\ns = Sale(\"Ahmed\", \"Jeddah\", 14000)\nprint(s.rep, s.amount)\n```\n(More in Lesson 34.)\n\n## 4. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Word frequency\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\ntext = \"the gel is good the drops are good the price is good\"\nprint(Counter(text.split()).most_common(3))   # [('the', 3), ('good', 3), ('is', 2)]\n```\n\n### Example 2: Group then summarise\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nsales = [(\"Jeddah\", \"Ahmed\", 14000), (\"Makkah\", \"Sara\", 9000), (\"Jeddah\", \"Omar\", 16000)]\nreps = defaultdict(list)\nfor region, rep, amount in sales:\n    reps[region].append(rep)\nfor region, names in reps.items():\n    print(region, \", \".join(names))\n```\n\n### Example 3: deque - a fast queue\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\nqueue = deque([\"Ahmed\", \"Sara\"])\nqueue.append(\"Omar\")          # add to the right\nqueue.appendleft(\"VIP\")       # add to the left\nprint(queue.popleft(), list(queue))   # VIP ['Ahmed', 'Sara', 'Omar']\nrecent = deque(maxlen=3)      # keeps only the last 3\nfor x in [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]:\n    recent.append(x)\nprint(list(recent))           # [3, 4, 5]\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> `Counter` replaces the \"dict.get(x, 0) + 1\" pattern; `defaultdict(list)` replaces \"setdefault(key, []).append(...)\". Both make grouping code shorter and clearer.\n\n## 5. Common mistakes\n- **Printing a defaultdict** shows `defaultdict(<class 'list'>, ...)` - wrap in `dict()` for clean output.\n- **Accessing a missing key creates it** in a defaultdict - check with `in` if you only want to read.\n- **Counter with negative counts** after subtraction - use `+counter` to drop them.\n\n## 6. Hands-on Student Challenge\n```python\nvisits = [\n    (\"Ahmed\", \"Al Nahdi\"), (\"Sara\", \"Al Dawaa\"), (\"Ahmed\", \"United\"),\n    (\"Omar\", \"Al Nahdi\"), (\"Ahmed\", \"Al Nahdi\"), (\"Sara\", \"Whites\"),\n]\n```\n1. Count visits per rep with Counter and print the top rep.\n2. Count visits per customer and show the 2 most visited.\n3. Group customers visited by each rep with defaultdict(set) (unique customers).\n4. Keep the last 3 visits in a deque.\n\n:::solution\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter, defaultdict, deque\n\nvisits = [(\"Ahmed\", \"Al Nahdi\"), (\"Sara\", \"Al Dawaa\"), (\"Ahmed\", \"United\"),\n          (\"Omar\", \"Al Nahdi\"), (\"Ahmed\", \"Al Nahdi\"), (\"Sara\", \"Whites\")]\n\nby_rep = Counter(rep for rep, _ in visits)\nprint(by_rep.most_common(1))                          # [('Ahmed', 3)]\n\nby_customer = Counter(c for _, c in visits)\nprint(by_customer.most_common(2))                     # [('Al Nahdi', 3), ('Al Dawaa', 1)]\n\ncustomers = defaultdict(set)\nfor rep, c in visits:\n    customers[rep].add(c)\nprint({rep: sorted(cs) for rep, cs in customers.items()})\n# {'Ahmed': ['Al Nahdi', 'United'], 'Sara': ['Al Dawaa', 'Whites'], 'Omar': ['Al Nahdi']}\n\nlast3 = deque(visits, maxlen=3)\nprint(list(last3))\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- `Counter` counts items and finds the most common.\n- `defaultdict` creates default values automatically for grouping.\n- `deque` is a fast queue; `namedtuple` makes readable records.\n",
    "tags": "Python, Lesson 95, Programming, Advanced",
