    "id": "py-97",
    "lesson_number": 97,
    "title": "Lesson 97: Deep Copy vs Shallow Copy with the copy Module",
    "slug": "python-lesson-97-deep-copy-vs-shallow-copy-with-the-copy-module",
    "category": "programming",
    "track": "python",
    "track_name": "Python Path",
    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Advanced",
    "summary": "Avoid unexpected mutations when copying complex nested data structures.",
    "content": "# Lesson 97: Deep Copy vs Shallow Copy with the copy Module\n\nAssigning a list or dict to another variable does **not** copy it - both names point to the same object. Understanding **shallow** and **deep** copies prevents some of the most confusing bugs in Python.\n\n## 1. Assignment is not copying\n```python\noriginal = [1, 2, 3]\nalias = original\nalias.append(4)\nprint(original)          # [1, 2, 3, 4] - same list!\nprint(alias is original) # True\n```\n\n## 2. Shallow copy\nA shallow copy creates a **new outer container**, but the items inside are the **same objects**.\n```python\nimport copy\noriginal = [1, 2, 3]\na = original.copy()      # or list(original), original[:], copy.copy(original)\na.append(4)\nprint(original)          # [1, 2, 3] - not affected\n```\nFine for lists of numbers/strings. But with nested objects:\n```python\nimport copy\nteam = [[\"Ahmed\", 14000], [\"Sara\", 9000]]\nshallow = copy.copy(team)\nshallow[0][1] = 99999\nprint(team[0])           # ['Ahmed', 99999] - the inner list is shared!\n```\n\n## 3. Deep copy\n`copy.deepcopy()` copies **everything**, all the way down:\n```python\nimport copy\nteam = [[\"Ahmed\", 14000], [\"Sara\", 9000]]\ndeep = copy.deepcopy(team)\ndeep[0][1] = 99999\nprint(team[0])           # ['Ahmed', 14000] - safe\n```\n\n## 4. Worked examples\n### Example 1: A template dictionary\n```python\nimport copy\ntemplate = {\"customer\": \"\", \"items\": [], \"status\": \"draft\"}\n\norder1 = copy.deepcopy(template)\norder1[\"items\"].append(\"Gel\")\norder2 = copy.deepcopy(template)\nprint(order2[\"items\"])     # [] - each order has its own list\n```\nWith `template.copy()` (shallow), both orders would share the same `items` list.\n\n### Example 2: The list multiplication trap\n```python\ngrid = [[0] * 3] * 3       # 3 references to the SAME row\ngrid[0][0] = 1\nprint(grid)                # [[1, 0, 0], [1, 0, 0], [1, 0, 0]]\n\ngrid = [[0] * 3 for _ in range(3)]   # 3 separate rows\ngrid[0][0] = 1\nprint(grid)                # [[1, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0]]\n```\n\n### Example 3: Functions changing your data\n```python\ndef add_vat_inplace(prices):\n    for i in range(len(prices)):\n        prices[i] *= 1.15\n\ndef add_vat_copy(prices):\n    return [p * 1.15 for p in prices]\n\np = [100, 200]\nnew = add_vat_copy(p)\nprint(p, new)              # original unchanged\nadd_vat_inplace(p)\nprint(p)                   # original changed!\n```\n\n### Example 4: Checking identity\n```python\na = [1, [2, 3]]\nb = a.copy()\nprint(a == b, a is b, a[1] is b[1])   # True False True\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Rule of thumb: for **flat** data (numbers, strings) a shallow copy is enough. For **nested** data (lists of lists, dicts of lists), use `copy.deepcopy()`.\n\n## 5. Common mistakes\n- **`b = a` thinking it copies.**\n- **Shallow copies of nested data.**\n- **`[[0] * n] * m`** for grids.\n\n## 6. Hands-on Student Challenge\n```python\nregions = {\"Jeddah\": {\"reps\": [\"Ahmed\", \"Omar\"], \"target\": 30000}, \"Makkah\": {\"reps\": [\"Sara\"], \"target\": 10000}}\n```\n1. Make a shallow copy, add a rep to Jeddah in the copy, and show the original was changed.\n2. Make a deep copy, change Makkah's target in the copy, and show the original is unchanged.\n3. Create a 3x4 grid of zeros correctly and set the centre-left cell to 5.\n\n:::solution\n```python\nimport copy\n\nregions = {\"Jeddah\": {\"reps\": [\"Ahmed\", \"Omar\"], \"target\": 30000}, \"Makkah\": {\"reps\": [\"Sara\"], \"target\": 10000}}\n\nshallow = regions.copy()\nshallow[\"Jeddah\"][\"reps\"].append(\"Lina\")\nprint(regions[\"Jeddah\"][\"reps\"])        # ['Ahmed', 'Omar', 'Lina'] - original changed!\n\ndeep = copy.deepcopy(regions)\ndeep[\"Makkah\"][\"target\"] = 12000\nprint(regions[\"Makkah\"][\"target\"], deep[\"Makkah\"][\"target\"])   # 10000 12000\n\ngrid = [[0] * 4 for _ in range(3)]\ngrid[1][0] = 5\nfor row in grid:\n    print(row)\n# [0, 0, 0, 0] / [5, 0, 0, 0] / [0, 0, 0, 0]\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- Assignment shares the same object.\n- Shallow copies duplicate only the outer container.\n- `copy.deepcopy()` duplicates nested data completely.\n",
    "tags": "Python, Lesson 97, Programming, Advanced",
    "read_time": 4,
    "author": "Mohammed Bargisy",
    "status": "published",
    "views": 129,
    "cover_gradient": "linear-gradient(135deg, #312e81, #8b5cf6)",
